{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And she conceived and bore Cain.</b> Its meaning is: and she bore a son and called his name Cain, for she said, \"I have acquired a man with YHWH.\" And likewise, \"and she bore Enoch\" (Genesis 4:17), and there are many instances in this section and in other places. <b>With YHWH</b> — together with YHWH: when He created me and my husband, He alone created us, but in this we are partners with Him. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 4:1). But the correct interpretation in my opinion is that she said: this son will be a possession for YHWH, for when we die he will be in our place to serve his Creator. And this is also the opinion of Onkelos (Targum Onkelos on Genesis 4:1), who rendered \"before YHWH,\" and similar to it (and it appears) \"and he shall show it to the priest\" (Leviticus 13:49) — to the priest; \"and David drew near to the people\" (I Samuel 30:21) — to the people. Or \"with YHWH\" may be like \"and Enoch walked with the Elohim\" (Genesis 5:22), \"Noah walked with the Elohim\" (Genesis 6:9). And she called the one by the name of a possession, and the second Abel, for man's possession is likened to vanity. And she did not wish to explain this, therefore no reason was written for the second name. And the received mystery concerning Abel is very great:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {0 "In the phrase 'and similar to it (and it appears),' the parenthetical ונראה appears to be a marginal gloss glossing the scriptural citation that follows."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:24:28.043231079Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}